Replay trust model
------------------
Replay does not trust a backend PASS string, mismatch count, aggregate timing, or output digest. It requires every flattened output row and locally recomputes the clear oracle, FNV-1a digest, mismatch count, compared-bit count, distinct signatures, and timing aggregates. Hardware strings are provenance only.

Negative controls exercised by DistinctLaneExchangeTests:
  - one output-bit corruption is recomputed as exactly one mismatch and timing_valid=false;
  - a stale output digest is rejected;
  - a wrong workload ID is rejected;
  - a missing lane is rejected;
  - a non-binary output row is rejected;
  - a tampered embedded netlist/workload is rejected.

Runnable workflow
-----------------
1. Build the shipped tools:
     swift build -c release

2. Export the exact workload without creating a Metal device:
     .build/release/helut-bench --bench Generated/Netlists/Examples/ripple4_netlist.json --bench-module ripple4 --degree 1 --batch 256 --ticks 6 --warmup 1 --bench-distinct-lanes --bench-distinct-export-workload logs/helut-discrete-gpu-contract-20260906T005547Z/workload.json

3. On another backend, consume workload.json exactly and emit helut.distinct-lane.result.v1 containing:
     - the unchanged workloadID;
     - one binary output row for every lane, in frozen output-port order;
     - all raw synchronized graph-run samples as integer nanoseconds;
     - backend/device provenance.
   No CUDA/HIP executable is claimed here because none exists in this repository. The JSON schema and shipped replay command are the runnable boundary.

4. Bring the result file back and run the replay command above. Only a locally replayed result with mismatches=0 and timing_valid=true is eligible for comparison. Compare raw synchronized-graph-run samples only between results carrying the same workload ID; do not mix compile, upload, packing, oracle, readback, or verification time into that graph timing.

The local Apple Metal result was generated with:
  .build/release/helut-bench --bench Generated/Netlists/Examples/ripple4_netlist.json --bench-module ripple4 --degree 1 --batch 256 --ticks 6 --warmup 1 --bench-distinct-lanes --bench-distinct-export-workload logs/helut-discrete-gpu-contract-20260906T005547Z/workload.json --bench-distinct-result-out logs/helut-discrete-gpu-contract-20260906T005547Z/metal-result.json

Exact blocker to an actual discrete comparison
----------------------------------------------
Available hardware is Apple M4 Max, 40 GPU cores, built-in/unified-memory, Metal 4. It is not a discrete NVIDIA or AMD GPU.

Tool discovery:
  nvcc: NOT_FOUND
  nvidia-smi: NOT_FOUND
  hipcc: NOT_FOUND
  rocminfo: NOT_FOUND

Production-source search for CUDA, HIP, ROCm, NVIDIA, AMD, and Vulkan found no corresponding execution backend. CUDA matches are comments referring to the external enigma-cuda project, not runnable HELUT backend code. Therefore this machine cannot produce an honest NVIDIA/AMD discrete result, and the repository has no production CUDA/HIP/ROCm/Vulkan runner to invoke elsewhere yet. Closing that final empirical gap requires both (a) implementing an independent backend that emits the frozen result schema and (b) running it on identified discrete hardware. Device-name strings alone are not accepted as attestation.

